<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Waipiʻo Valley</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Waipio Valley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Waipiʻo Valley is a deep valley on the north-east coast of the island of Hawaiʻi, in the Hāmākua district. Watered by streams and waterfalls, it held extensive taro fields and was a seat of the island's rulers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liloa">Līloa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umi-a-liloa">ʻUmi-a-Līloa</a>, and of the sacred temple and refuge of Pākaʻalana. Its royal burial and religious sites give it great importance in Hawaiian tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/waipio-valley.jpg" alt="Waipiʻo Valley" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Hirst (Phirst) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waipio_Lookout_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
